--TEST--
Core\Arr::sortByKey compares a numeral key bytewise where ksort reads it as a number, so "10" precedes "9" here and follows it there
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
[ADR 0007](../../../docs/adr/0007-explicit-type-system.md) § 5 makes every stored key a `string`, so `sortByKey` has one kind of thing to order and orders it the one way `nvs_stdlib::ordering::compare_values` orders two strings: bytewise. PHP arrives at the same sort by two conversions Novis does not have. It canonicalizes a decimal integer key to an `int` on the way in — `$a["9"]` and `$a[9]` are one entry, and `array_keys` answers `9` — and then `ksort`'s default `SORT_REGULAR` compares two numbers numerically and, since PHP 8, compares two *numeral strings* numerically as well. So `"9"` precedes `"10"` there and follows it here, and `"50"` precedes `"1e2"` there and follows it here — the second pair being the same numeral-string comparison `Core\Arr::min` already diverges over in `arr-min-and-max-diverge-from-php-over-an-empty-mixed-or-numeric-string-subject`. Reproducing PHP's rule would mean a key's ordering depended on how it was spelled rather than on what it is, which is the changes-type-by-itself reading `nvs_core_arr_sort` rejects for values; a key sort is the same question, and `nvs_core_arr_sort_by_key`'s doc comment owns that reasoning. The way round is the `comparator` option, asserted in the last row below: it is `ksort`'s answer exactly, written by a caller who says numeric order is what they meant. The agreeing half — every subject whose keys PHP cannot read as numbers — is asserted against PHP itself in `arr-sort-by-key-on-non-numeral-keys-matches-ksort`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Render {
    public static function keys(array<mixed> $a): string {
        return Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys($a), ",");
    }
}

// Three decimal integer keys. `ksort` answers `9,10,100`, having read each as
// a number; the byte compare answers the lexicographic order, in which a
// shorter numeral is not the smaller one.
array<string> $counts = ["10" => "ten", "9" => "nine", "100" => "hundred"];
echo Render::keys(Core\Arr::sortByKey($counts)), "\n";

// The three spellings PHP 8 compares numerically *as strings*, none of which
// it canonicalizes to an int key: `0.5 < 50 < 1e2` gives `0.5,50,1e2` there.
// Bytewise, `"1e2"` falls between them, because `1` precedes `5`.
array<string> $numerals = ["1e2" => "a", "50" => "b", "0.5" => "c"];
echo Render::keys(Core\Arr::sortByKey($numerals)), "\n";

// A mixed subject shows there is no second rule here to arbitrate: PHP sorts
// its two word keys against its numeral keys by `strcmp` and its numeral keys
// against each other numerically, so `2,9,10` leads and Novis's `10,2,9` does
// not, while the two words land in the same place on both sides.
array<int> $mixed = ["10" => 1, "9" => 2, "banana" => 3, "Apple" => 4, "2" => 5];
echo Render::keys(Core\Arr::sortByKey($mixed)), "\n";

// The way round, and the reason the divergence is a default rather than a
// refusal: a caller who wants numeric order says so, and this row is `ksort`'s
// answer to the first subject above, byte for byte.
echo Render::keys(Core\Arr::sortByKey($counts, {comparator: fn(string $a, string $b): int => ($a as int) - ($b as int)})), "\n";
--EXPECT--
10,100,9
0.5,1e2,50
10,2,9,Apple,banana
9,10,100
